H-ACE 2035

Human-Centered Adaptive Correctional Ecosystem

A Systems Engineering Framework for Transitional Corrections

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Date: 2026-08-20
Status: Final PhD Dissertation Draft — Theoretical Framework
Version: 1.0 — Systems Engineering and Governance Specification

---

Abstract

This dissertation presents H-ACE 2035, a Human-Centered Adaptive Correctional Ecosystem designed as a socio-technical alternative to binary incarceration. The framework treats correctional environments not as closed buildings but as engineered transition systems that move individuals from restricted liberty toward autonomous community participation under graded security, dignity, and evidence-based rehabilitation constraints.

H-ACE 2035 is governed by a single normative principle:

Maximum security, minimum surveillance.

The thesis develops the systems engineering, architecture, AI governance, privacy, security, resilience, human factors, and validation framework for such an ecosystem. It introduces a Human-Governed Adaptive Autonomy Framework, a Portable Skills & Achievement Credential, and a Last Kilometre Village transition campus. No empirical implementation or code-level training has been conducted; the work is theoretical and specification-oriented, designed to be experimentally testable.

The central contribution is the formalization of correction as a controlled transition process rather than a release event.

