3.3 Architectural Principles

1. Layer separation
      Security, rehabilitation, clinical, and administrative services shall be architecturally separated.
2. Human accountability
      Automated systems may recommend. Humans shall decide consequential matters.
3. Minimum surveillance
      Data collection shall be necessary, proportionate, and limited.
4. Privacy by architecture
      The architecture shall make unauthorized surveillance technically difficult, not merely procedurally discouraged.
5. Resilience by redundancy
      Critical systems shall not have single points of failure.
6. Interoperability
      Systems shall use documented interfaces and open standards where feasible.
7. Auditability
      Every consequential decision, override, and data access event shall be logged.

3.4 Interface Types

Interface Description
Human–AI Decision support, alerts, educational assistance
Human–Robot Supervised service interaction
Human–Building Environmental control, access, emergency
AI–AI Restricted, mediated data exchange only
Digital Twin–Physical Real-time monitoring and simulation
Governance–All Audit, inspection, policy enforcement

No interface shall permit direct AI control over physical restraint, medication, solitary confinement, or involuntary movement.

