Chapter 7

Rehabilitation Intelligence and the Portable Skills & Achievement Credential

7.1 Scope

Rehabilitation Intelligence shall be a support system, not a control system. It shall focus on education, employability, health literacy, legal literacy, release readiness, and community navigation.

7.2 PSAC as a Verifiable Credential

The former “Rehabilitation Ledger” is replaced by the Portable Skills & Achievement Credential (PSAC).

PSAC shall be designed as a user-controlled verifiable credential compatible with the W3C Verifiable Credentials model.

Formally:

\text{PSAC} = (\text{Holder DID}, \text{Schema}, \text{Issuer}, \text{Claims}, \text{Proofs})

where:

\text{Claims}_{\text{PSAC}} \subseteq \{\text{skills}, \text{certificates}, \text{work hours}, \text{training}, \text{digital literacy}, \text{safety training}, \text{professional qualification}\}

and the following constraint holds:

\text{Claims}_{\text{PSAC}} \cap \{\text{criminal history}, \text{behavioral score}, \text{predictive risk score}, \text{emotional score}\} = \emptyset

PSAC records verified positive achievements only. It is portable, verifiable, and user-controlled for sharing.

The person controls what is shared, with whom, and for what purpose.

7.3 Consent and Data Minimization

Rehabilitation Intelligence shall operate on informed consent where legally possible, minimize data collection, separate rehabilitation data from security data, and allow correction and deletion of inaccurate records.

