8.2 Privacy Principles

The architecture shall implement lawfulness, purpose limitation, data minimization, storage limitation, integrity, confidentiality, and accountability.

8.3 Precise Privacy Red Lines

The following definitions shall apply:

· Biometric surveillance: collection or processing of physiological or behavioral characteristics for identification, tracking, or inference.
· Continuous: more than one sample per hour in a private or semi-private space without explicit consent.
· Hidden microphone array: any audio capture device not visibly disclosed and not required for emergency communication.

The following shall be prohibited:

· hidden microphone arrays;
· emotion recognition;
· thought-prediction;
· continuous biometric surveillance;
· monitoring of family conversations;
· predictive risk scores used as identity or fixed destiny.

8.4 Emergency Clinical Data Sharing

In a documented emergency, clinical data may be shared with security staff only if the clinician on duty determines that disclosure is necessary to prevent serious harm.

The disclosure shall be:

· logged;
· time-limited;
· reviewed by the medical director;
· reviewed by the independent oversight body.

This protects the ethical firewall while allowing lawful emergency response.

8.5 Retention and Deletion

· Data at rest and in transit shall be encrypted.
· High-sensitivity data shall be held in on-premises sovereign infrastructure where legally required.
· Retention periods shall be defined by law.
· Non-relevant data shall be automatically deleted.

