Chapter 13

Human-Governed Adaptive Autonomy Framework

13.1 Conceptual Correction

The former “Progression Engine” is replaced by the Human-Governed Adaptive Autonomy Framework. This is not an AI progression algorithm.

13.2 Governance Function

Autonomy level shall be determined by a human governance function:

A = G(R, P, B, L, C, O)

where:

Variable Meaning
R Security risk evidence
P Rehabilitation progress
B Behavioral stability
L Legal constraints
C Clinical input
O Oversight, procedural rights, appeal
G Multidisciplinary human decision function

AI may provide evidence for R, P, or B, but G is human and institutional.

13.3 Human Autonomy Review Board

The governance function G shall be implemented by a Human Autonomy Review Board (HARB).

HARB membership shall include:

· correctional professional;
· psychologist or clinician where relevant;
· educator;
· case manager;
· legal representative;
· independent oversight representative;
· the person concerned.

AI may supply evidence; HARB makes the decision.

13.4 Procedural Rights

Each autonomy review shall include:

· notice to the resident;
· access to the evidence used;
· legal representation;
· written explanation;
· appeal to an independent body;
· periodic review;
· audit trail.

13.5 Risk Score Governance

Any security risk score shall be validated, transparent, contestable, periodically recalibrated, and independently reviewed for bias. Risk scores shall not be treated as identity or fixed destiny.

13.6 Red Lines

The following shall not be decided solely by AI:

· guilt;
· punishment;
· parole;
· solitary confinement;
· medical treatment;
· fundamental rights.

