Chapter 14

Last Kilometre Village and Transition Engineering

14.1 Transition as an Engineering Problem

The final stage of incarceration is the most important for public safety.

Conventional systems treat release as an event. H-ACE treats release as a transition process.

Formally:

\text{Release}_{\text{conventional}} = \text{Event}

\text{Release}_{\text{H-ACE}} = \text{Process}

14.2 Transition Campus

The Last Kilometre Village operates outside the hard perimeter, under digital and human oversight.

It includes:

· small apartments;
· shared kitchens;
· financial management training;
· public transport training;
· employment offices;
· community organization access;
· family meeting spaces;
· digital service points;
· psychological support;
· supervised external work.

14.3 Transition Readiness Vector

Readiness shall not be a single score.

T = \{H, E, M, S, F, D, C\}

where:

Symbol Dimension
H Housing
E Employment
M Medical continuity
S Social support
F Financial readiness
D Documentation
C Community integration

No composite “freedom score” shall be computed. Each dimension has independent evidence and human verification.

14.4 Transition Phases

Phase Description
T0 Initial transition assessment at admission
T1 Pre-transition within secure campus
T2 Transition campus under digital/human oversight
T3 Community supervision and support

14.5 AI Companion

During transition, Rehabilitation Intelligence becomes a release companion.

It shall ask:

“You have an appointment tomorrow at 14:00. Do you need help preparing your route or documents?”

It shall not simply ask:

“Where are you?”

14.6 Break-Glass Protocol

A person may voluntarily return to a supervised unit for one night if they feel they are about to fail.

This is not a sanction.

The break-glass event shall be:

· non-punitive;
· not a rule violation;
· not used as evidence of instability;
· not used by parole boards or judges as negative evidence.

