Appendix D

AI Governance Formal Rules and Decision Logic

D.1 Definitions

· Consequential AI Output: Any AI output that could affect security level, privileges, restraint, medication, release, or legal status.
· Override: A human decision that rejects or modifies an AI recommendation.

D.2 Required Metadata

Every consequential AI output O shall be a tuple:

O = \langle \text{ModelVersion}, \text{InputCategory}, \text{Confidence}, \text{HumanDecision}, \text{OverrideReason}, \text{OutcomeLog} \rangle

Where:

· \text{ModelVersion} is the exact model identifier.
· \text{InputCategory} is the data category used.
· \text{Confidence} is a calibrated probability where applicable.
· \text{HumanDecision} is the final human action.
· \text{OverrideReason} is non-empty when human decision differs from recommendation.
· \text{OutcomeLog} is the observed result for later review.

D.3 Formal Rule

\forall O \in \text{ConsequentialOutputs}: \text{HumanDecision}(O) \neq \emptyset

\forall O: \text{OverrideReason}(O) = \emptyset \implies \text{HumanDecision}(O) = \text{AIRecommendation}(O)

D.4 Proof of No AI-Only Control

Theorem: In the H-ACE architecture, no high-impact correctional decision can be enacted solely by AI.

Proof:

1. Let D be a high-impact decision.
2. By HACE-AI-001, D requires human sign-off.
3. By C.3 state transition model, D cannot transition to final state without h \in \text{HumanDecisionMakers}.
4. The interface control matrix prohibits any AI system from writing to physical restraint, medication, solitary confinement, or involuntary movement actuators.
5. Therefore, there exists no execution path from AI recommendation to D enactment without human intervention.
6. Hence, no high-impact decision is made solely by AI. \square

