Appendix H

Resiliency and Environmental Load Calculations (72-Hour Requirement)

H.1 Decomposition

The 72-hour resilience requirement HACE-ENV-001 is decomposed into subsystems:

Subsystem Load/Target Method
Electricity Baseline load × 72 h Battery + renewable + backup generator
Potable water Per-person daily × 72 h Storage tanks
Wastewater Storage or treatment capacity Holding tanks / treatment
HVAC Maintain 20\text{--}26^\circ\text{C} Redundant air handlers, passive design
Medical refrigeration 2\text{--}8^\circ\text{C} for 72 h Dedicated battery
Communications Voice/data 72 h Backup power, satellite
Food 72 h non-perishable Storage
Cybersecurity Logs and access control 72 h UPS + secure offline
Emergency lighting 72 h Battery
Fire systems 72 h Battery + backup
Access control 72 h Battery + manual override

H.2 Sample Electrical Load Calculation

Assume pilot module baseline load P_{\text{base}} = 40 \text{ kW}.

For 72 hours:

E_{\text{need}} = P_{\text{base}} \times 72 = 40 \times 72 = 2880 \text{ kWh}

Battery capacity required:

E_{\text{battery}} \ge \frac{E_{\text{need}}}{\eta_{\text{discharge}}}

Assuming \eta_{\text{discharge}} = 0.9:

E_{\text{battery}} \ge \frac{2880}{0.9} \approx 3200 \text{ kWh}

Renewable generation can reduce storage requirement. A microgrid controller shall optimize dispatch.

H.3 Probability of Operational Continuity

For each subsystem i, define:

P_i(72\text{h}) = 1 - P(\text{failure}_i)

The total probability of critical continuity under grid failure, assuming independence:

P_{\text{total}}(72\text{h}) = \prod_{i \in \text{critical}} P_i(72\text{h})

The acceptance criterion P_{\text{total}} \ge 0.99 implies each subsystem must meet high reliability. This is a design target verified by simulation and fault injection.

